    "https://jobs.wichita.edu/jobs/postdoctoral-researcher-forensic-psychology-wichita-kansas-united-states": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher, Forensic Psychology",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Researcher, Forensic Psychology - Wichita, Kansas, United States Postdoctoral Researcher, Forensic Psychology - Wichita, Kansas, United States social--blog Kiosk mode Postdoctoral Researcher, Forensic Psychology College of Liberal Arts & Sciences | Staff Postdoctoral Researcher, Forensic Psychology 499816 Staff Department: Department of Psychology Campus Location: Wichita, KS - WSU Main Campus Hire Type: Full Time Pay: $60,000 Work Schedule: Monday - Friday, 8 - 5; work is performed on-site and in person. Export Compliance Requirement: No export control requirement. Job Story The Forensic Psychology Postdoctoral Residency Program offers a comprehensive, clinically intensive structured training experience designed for clinical psychology doctoral graduates who are committed to specializing in the application of psychological science to legal questions. Grounded in scientist-practitioner principles, the residency integrates advanced forensic assessment, empirical research, professional mentorship, and engagement with the legal and justice communities. Postdoctoral residents engage in an array of clinical, research, supervisory, and didactic activities within a collaborative university-based forensic psychology training environment. This position is designed to prepare early-career psychologists for independent licensure and a successful career at the intersection of psychology and the law. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue on a rolling basis until position are filled. Interviews will be conducted via video conferencing. Salary is $60,000.00. Job Summary Administers a variety of studies within scientific or scholarly area of expertise. Performs research study analyses, writes reports and publications, and coordinates with others in carrying out research. Essential Functions Participates with others on research projects. Builds upon previous or concurrent research. Develops research projects through a variety of methods, collaborations and analyses. Gathers data and develops statistical analyses to assimilate and interpret research results. Resolves data and/or research issues. Researches, writes, and communicates research progress and outcomes. Maintains research facility data, equipment, and specimens to ensure compliance with applicable rules and regulations. May supervise the work of others. Job Duties A. Forensic Clinical Assessment The core focus of the residency is the conduct of high-quality, evidence-based forensic psychological evaluations. Residents will develop and refine expertise across a range of court-ordered, agency-referred, and specialized assessment contexts, such as: • Pre-employment psychological evaluations of law enforcement officer candidates • Fitness-for-duty evaluations of active law enforcement and public safety personnel • Competency to stand trial evaluations • Criminal responsibility evaluations • Psychological risk assessments (e.g., violence risk, sex offender risk, recidivism) • Psychological mitigation evaluations for criminal sentencing, capital cases, and related proceedings • Other court-ordered or attorney-referred forensic evaluations as indicated Residents will be expected to produce written forensic psychological reports that meet professional and legal standards and may be called upon to provide expert testimony in judicial or administrative proceedings under supervision. B. Community Engagement and Program Development To support program sustainability and the development of intraprofessional forensic psychology competencies, residents will assist in building and maintaining collaborative relationships with key referral sources and community stakeholders, such as: • Law enforcement agencies and public safety departments • County and state courts (criminal, family, juvenile) • Prosecutorial and defense attorney offices • Correctional institutions and diversion programs • Community mental health and crisis intervention agencies Residents will represent the program professionally in agency meetings, consultations, and community outreach efforts. C. Research Participation Residents are encouraged to engage in empirical research as part of their professional development. Research activities may include: • Collaboration on active research projects within the program's affiliated research laboratory • Design and conduct of original studies in forensic psychology • Preparation and submission of peer-reviewed manuscripts and conference presentations • Assistance with IRB protocols, data collection and management, and statistical analysis Research participation is strongly encouraged but may be adjusted based on clinical volume and program needs. D. Supervision of Clinical Trainees At the discretion of program supervisors and with approval of other relevant individuals, residents may provide direct supervision and mentorship to doctoral practicum students engaged in clinical psychology training. Supervisory responsibilities may include: • Individual and group supervision of assessment cases • Case conceptualization guidance and feedback on written reports • Modeling of professional and ethical conduct in practice • Participation in didactic training activities, peer consultation, and case conferences E. Teaching Residents who have an interest in academic careers may elect to teach one or more courses in forensic psychology, subject to departmental availability and approval. Teaching opportunities may include: • Undergraduate survey courses in forensic psychology or psychology and law • Graduate-level seminars in forensic assessment or specialized psychological topics • Guest lectures in related courses Required Education and Experience: Doctorate degree in a related field One (1) year of experience in research, laboratory, scholarly setting, or related field Required License/Certifications/Training: None Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Ability to work collaboratively in a professional office environment to include, but not limited to; knowledge of office ethics and proper office behavior; produce at a high level independently and in teams; adapt quickly to change and feedback; problem solve and use independent judgement. Ability to exercise appropriate personal initiative and accountability with exceptional follow through, attention to details, and track and meet deadlines. Strong time management, organizational and record keeping skills. Strong written and verbal communication skills Ability to consistently and positively interact with colleagues and other constituents Be license-eligible Preferred Qualifications: A doctorate from an APA-accredited clinical psychology doctoral program. Additional Information: To apply, please complete the application and be prepared to upload the following materials: Cover letter describing your interest in forensic psychology, relevant training background, and career goals Curriculum vitae Graduate transcripts (unofficial accepted for initial review) Two to three de-identified writing samples (e.g., psychological reports, research papers) In addition, please arrange for three (3) letters of recommendation (at least one from a clinical supervisor) to be sent to Dr. Brendan Clark @ c.brendan.clark@wichita.edu WSU is not able to provide immigration sponsorship for this position. Physical Requirements: Ability to remain in a stationary position. Ability to operate a computer and other office equipment including but not limited to phone, printers, copiers, and calculators. Ability to communicate with others and accurately exchange information. Ability to use tools, equipment, and technology necessary to do the job. This includes dexterity in instrument manipulation. Ability to move, transport, and/or deliver items, supplies, or equipment. Additional Physical Requirement: Sedentary: Exerting up to 10 pounds of force occasionally and/or a negligible amount of force frequently or constantly to lift, carry, push, pull or otherwise move objects. Sedentary work involves remaining stationary most of the time. Apply Now First Name (required) 6580b732 Last Name (required) 2087da33 Email (required) 41f2b694 Apply Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form and including your phone number, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about working at Wichita State University. Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency varies. Unsubscribe at any time by replying STOP or clicking the unsubscribe link (where available). Privacy Policy Thank you Sign Up for Job Alerts! First Name (required) 38f4d3f5 Last Name (required) 059f7e12 Email (required) ce9b982b Job Alert a3e19ee8 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Staff Staff Department(s) College of Liberal Arts & Sciences College of Liberal Arts & Sciences Send Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form and including your phone number, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about working at Wichita State University. Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency varies. Unsubscribe at any time by replying STOP or clicking the unsubscribe link (where available). Privacy Policy Thank you Share This Job With Someone Else! First Name (required) 184210e0 Last Name (required) 7bfe49ab Email (required) 2d70c3f0 Send This Job To: 14f2fb3a Share Now! Please wait... Done Not You? By submitting this form and including your phone number, you consent to receive text messages related to available jobs and information about working at Wichita State University. Message & data rates may apply. Message frequency varies. Unsubscribe at any time by replying STOP or clicking the unsubscribe link (where available). Privacy Policy We've sent this job to the email provided! No